{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 38, :comments ["<b>Speak to the children of Israel.</b> The commandment of tzitzit in general terms. <b>And you shall say to them,</b> the particulars of the commandment. And so it is with all the sections concerning the other commandments where it says 'speak to the children of Israel' and 'and you shall say to them' — the meaning is thus, for every commandment has a general statement and particulars, and I have already mentioned this at the beginning of the section of Vayikra." "<b>And they shall make for themselves tzitzit.</b> Named for the four threads suspended from it, as in (Ezekiel 8:3) 'ויקחני בציצת ראשי.' 'And they shall make for themselves tzitzit' — and not from that which is already made, meaning one may not pull threads out of the garment and make tzitzit from them. And how do you make the tzitzit, and how many threads do you put in it? Four: you insert four threads through the hole of the garment and fold them in the middle, and they become eight, and you place them within three fingerbreadths of the corner of the garment. And one must distance the placement of the tzitzit from the edge of the garment by the size of a thumb-knot, which is the measure of a fingernail and flesh up to the first joint. And the length of the threads at the outset should be no less than four fingerbreadths, but if they later diminished and only enough remains to tie them, and likewise if the corners of the garment between the place where the threads are fastened and the end of the weaving diminished — even if only the slightest amount of the weaving remains, it is valid. But if even a single thread breaks off at its root, it is invalid. And one takes one of the threads and wraps it around the remaining threads for a third of their length, and this is what is called the gedil, and one leaves two-thirds as a free fringe. And wrapping the thread three times around the other threads is called a khulya, and one may make no fewer than seven khulyot in each corner, and one may add no more than thirteen, corresponding to the seven heavens and the six airy spaces between them. And because the tekhelet resembles the sea and the sea resembles the heavens, we liken its arrangements to the arrangements of the heavens, in order to remind us for good." "<b>Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote</b> concerning the count of threads, which is eight: that seven are white — meaning undyed — and the eighth is tekhelet, because it says 'פתיל תכלת,' implying one. And this tekhelet is a thread of wool dyed in the blood of the ḥillazon, which is a fish whose eye resembles the sea, and its blood is black like ink, and it is found in the Salt Sea. One first soaks the wool in lime and then washes it until it is clean, and one casts it into a boiling cauldron of that blood together with the wool along with dyestuffs such as alum and cassis in the manner that dyers do, so that the wool absorbs the color. And some say two threads of tekhelet and six of white. And how does one do it? One takes one of the white threads and wraps a single wrapping around the rest of the threads at the side of the garment and leaves it, then takes the tekhelet thread and wraps two windings next to the winding of white and ties. And these three windings are called a khulya as we said, and this is called the upper knot. One distances a bit and makes a second khulya with the tekhelet thread alone, and distances a bit and makes a third khulya, and so on until the last khulya, in which one wraps two windings of tekhelet and a final winding of white, because since one began with white one concludes with it, as it is written 'ונתנו על ציצית הכנף פתיל תכלת,' and our Sages, of blessed memory, said: 'הכנף מין כנף' — it must be adjacent to the corner of the same kind, and afterward 'פתיל תכלת,' and one concludes with white because we ascend in holiness and do not descend, and since Scripture gave precedence to 'מין כנף,' learn from it that it is the more distinguished, and if one were to conclude with tekhelet it would be a descent. And since the commandment of tekhelet requires that the entire thread be tekhelet, one does not make with white except a single winding alone, adjacent to the corner of the same kind, and the rest of that khulya along with all the khulyot is in tekhelet, except for the final winding which is in white since one began with it. And so the Geonim, of blessed memory, wrote in their compositions and in their responsa. And now that we do not have tekhelet, we make white without tekhelet, for we hold: tekhelet does not prevent the white, and likewise the white does not prevent the tekhelet — meaning that if one made white and tekhelet, and the white broke and diminished up to the corner and only the tekhelet remained, it is valid. And the white refers to the fringe threads that one makes on the corner of the garment from the same material as the garment. And it is called tzitzit because it resembles the tzitzit of the head, as it is written (Ezekiel 8:3) 'ויקחני בציצת ראשי,' and this fringe is called white because we are not commanded to dye it. And even though neither one prevents the other, they are not two commandments but one commandment, and one who wears a garment that has white or tekhelet or both together has fulfilled one positive commandment, because it says 'ונתנו על ציצית הכנף פתיל תכלת' and 'והיה לכם לציצית' — teaching that both of them are one commandment. And it was taught: Rabbi Meir used to say — the punishment for white, when one fails to put it on, is as severe as the punishment for tekhelet. A parable: a king of flesh and blood said to his servants, 'Bring me two signet rings' — for they would make a signet ring for a servant as a sign of servitude when acquiring him. To one he said, a gold ring; to the other he said, a clay ring. Both transgressed and did not bring them. Whose punishment is greater? You would say this one who was told a clay ring, since it is readily available and he had no reason to refrain. And so too one who fails to put on white, which is more available than tekhelet. And now that we make everything white, one takes one of the eight threads and wraps it for a third of their length and leaves two-thirds of them as a fringe. And this wrapping — if one wishes to wrap it khulya by khulya in the manner one wraps with tekhelet, he may do so, and this is our custom. And if one wishes to wrap without a count of khulyot, he may do so. The bottom line is: one should intend that the wrapped portion be one-third and at the top, and the fringe two-thirds and at the bottom. And so our Sages, of blessed memory, said regarding a mezuzah, it is a commandment to place it in the upper third. And the reason we make five knots is because they said: the commandment of tzitzit is equivalent to the entire Torah, for all the commandments, which are 613 (תרי״ג), are alluded to in the commandment of tzitzit. 'ציצי״ת' in gematria is 600 (ת״ר), and eight threads and five knots are 13, giving 613 (תרי״ג). And this is what is written: 'וראיתם אותו וזכרתם את כל מצות ה׳.' And do not challenge me from the word 'ציצית' mentioned three times in the section, which is spelled deficiently, 'ציצת' without a yod, for there is one who says that the lamed of 'לציצית' comes to complete the three yods." "<b>And the reason</b> it mentions it three times in the section corresponds to three commandments, each of which is equivalent to the entire Torah, and they are: idolatry, Sabbath, and tzitzit — and this is the order of the sections. And Rashi, of blessed memory, wrote: the eight threads in it correspond to the eight days that Israel waited from when they left Egypt until they said the song at the sea — end of quote. And it is a clear matter that on the seventh day of Passover Israel said the song at the sea and not on the eighth. But the explanation of his words is: 'from when they left Egypt' means from the hour they were given permission, and the fourteenth day, the day of slaughtering the Paschal offering, is included in this count, because the Paschal offering is consecrated food, and for consecrated food the day does not follow the night — as it is written (Leviticus 7:15) 'ביום קרבנו יאכל לא יניח ממנו עד בקר' — but the night is fitting for it. Therefore, when Israel said the song at the sea, it was the seventh day of the days of Passover and the eighth day counting from the exodus from Egypt from the hour they were given permission, which is the night of the fifteenth, which belongs to the preceding day, the fourteenth." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> 'And they shall make for themselves tzitzit' — tzitzit is a term of seeing, from the language of (Song of Songs 2:9) 'מציץ מן החרכים.' And one who wears tzitzit must be careful of transgressions, for the Throne of Glory sees him — for it resembles tekhelet. And in the language of the Sages it is called 'טלית,' and this is from the language of elevation and exaltation, from the language of (Daniel 7:4) 'ונטילת מן ארעא' — and this alludes to the Holy One, blessed be He, who is elevated and exalted above all. And we were commanded to wrap ourselves in it from what they expounded on (Exodus 34:6) 'ויעבור ה׳ על פניו' — were it not written in Scripture it would be impossible to say it, as it were: this teaches that the Holy One, blessed be He, wrapped Himself like a prayer leader and showed Himself to Moses at Sinai and said to him: whenever Israel sins, perform before Me in this order and I will forgive them." "<b>And the intention</b> in this utterance is to teach us the order of prayer and supplication — how we entreat before Him: that we wrap ourselves in a tallit and recall before Him the thirteen attributes with intention, and He will thereby forgive us. And when he says 'we wrap ourselves,' the meaning is in a white tallit, which is a sign of forgiveness and atonement; just as the red thing is a sign of sin, so white is a sign of pardon, as it is written (Isaiah 1:18): ״אם יהיו חטאיכם כשנים כשלג ילבינו אם יאדימו כתולע כצמר יהיו״. And that a person puts on the tallit and covers himself with it alludes to the Holy One, blessed be He, that He covers His creatures and clothes them, in the manner of what is written (Genesis 3:21): ״ויעש ה׳ אלהים לאדם ולאשתו כתנות עור וילבישם״; and it also alludes to the future, that the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to revive the dead and raise them in their garments, as it is written (Job 38:14): ״תתהפך כחומר חותם ויתיצבו כמו לבוש״. And since this commandment alludes to the resurrection of the human body, you will find that it encompasses the entire body: for the ציצית are threads suspended in resemblance to the hair of the head, as it is written (Ezekiel 8:3): ״ויקחני בציצת ראשי״; the holes that are made in the tallit to suspend the threads in them are in resemblance to the eyes; and five knots correspond to the five senses within him; eight threads correspond to the eighth day on which he is circumcised; and from this they said that whoever is not circumcised does not merit the resurrection of the dead. And in each corner you will find five knots, making twenty knots for four corners, corresponding to the hands and the feet, which have twenty digits among them — and behold, you have the totality of the body. And the fact that of each corner that emerges from the holes of the tallit, one third is bound and wrapped together as one above and two thirds are spread and divided one from another below — the matter alludes to that even though Israel is now scattered in exile among the nations and they are in the lower rank, in the time of redemption they are destined to have the upper hand, as He said (Deuteronomy 26:19): ״ולתתך עליון על כל גויי הארץ״. And the prophet already alluded to this matter, saying (Zechariah 13:8): ״והיה בכל הארץ נאם ה׳ פי שנים בה יכרתו יגועו והשלישית יותר בה״; and they said in the Midrash: ״והשלישית יותר בה״ — these are Israel, as it is said (Isaiah 19:24): ״והיה ביום ההוא יהיה ישראל שלישיה״. And they further expounded: whoever fulfills the commandment of ציצית merits and is served by two thousand and eight hundred servants, as it is said (Zechariah 8:23): ״והיה ביום ההוא והחזיקו עשרה אנשים מכל לשונות הגוים בכנף איש יהודי לאמר נלכה עמכם כי שמענו אלהים עמכם״ — the seventy nations are ten men from each nation, making seven hundred for one corner, and for four corners two thousand and eight hundred; and whoever disdains the commandment of ציצית, concerning him Scripture says (Job 38:13): ״לאחוז בכנפות הארץ וינערו רשעים ממנה״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah, ״ועשו להם ציצית״ — this commandment, with its thirty-two threads, alludes to the Bride of the Song of Songs, who is wrapped and comprised of thirty-two wondrous paths of wisdom; and concerning her He mentioned there (Song of Songs 4:11): ״וריח שלמותיך כריח לבנון״; and it is known that there is no completion of a thing without her, as they expounded, may their memory be for a blessing, in Bereshit Rabbah (Daniel 12:6): ״ויאמר לאיש לבוש הבדים״ — like this one whose garment is from Him; and therefore we were commanded in the wrapping of the tallit with thirty-two threads because it is said (Deuteronomy 28:9): ״והלכת בדרכיו״; and this commandment is equivalent to all the commandments in the Torah, because it is the cornerstone that comprises תרי״ג precious stones and thirty-two paths, and each path divides into two — good and evil, in זכור and שמור, positive commandments and negative commandments — and behold they are ס״ד, and each one is comprised of ten sefirot, making תר״ם; remove from them the כ״ז letters of the Torah, which are the תרי״ג commandments. And this is what they said in the tractate Shavuot: ״וראיתם אותו וזכרתם את כל מצות ה׳״ — the commandment of ציצית is equivalent to all the commandments together. Thus wrote the sage Rabbi Azriel, of blessed memory, in his commentary on the Song of Songs that he composed." "<b>And further,</b> in this commandment there is a wondrous secret, and it is that through it Israel liken themselves to the חיות המרכבה. And you already know that all the commandments are of two kinds, received (מקובלות) and rational (מושכלות), and the sages of truth, of blessed memory, established for us the formula of blessings for the received commandments but did not establish them for the rational commandments, because the received ones are the very core of holiness, and on account of them we are called holy; therefore they established for them a blessing to say: 'who has sanctified us with His commandments and commanded us.'" "<b>And it is known</b> to the discerning that the received commandments that we were commanded to perform with physical implements all allude to and are a model of intelligible matters. And behold, the sages, of blessed memory, likened a person standing in prayer before HaShem, may He be exalted, to the holy חיות — and this is what they said, of blessed memory: 'one who prays must align his feet,' as it is said (Ezekiel 1:7): ״ורגליהם רגל ישרה״ — they brought proof for the one who prays from the holy חיות." "<b>From now on,</b> hear the explanation of this commandment, through which Israel liken themselves to the holy חיות. Know that there are four חיות that bear the Glory and serve as His מרכבה, and therefore they are called חיות המרכבה, and all four are but a single חיה; and the reason four חיות were created is on account of the existence of the cloud and the darkness and the other appearances that surround it. And behold, this is in the likeness of the tree: for the essence of the tree and its body are one, while the branches are many, dividing one from another, even though all are from a single root. And therefore Scripture mentioned (ibid.): ״ודמות על ראשי החיה רקיע כעין הקרח הנורא״ and so on — for after it had mentioned four חיות, it went back and referred to them as a single חיה, and did not say 'על ראשי החיות.' And behold, each חיה of the four חיות has four wings, making sixteen wings for the four חיות; and each one has four faces, making sixteen faces for the four חיות, as it is written (ibid.): ״וארבעה פנים לאחת וארבע כנפים לאחת להם״. And behold, they are ל״ב between the wings and the faces; and all of this has roots and mighty meanings." "<b>And according to the opinion.</b> Jonathan in his Targum counts the faces of the four Ḥayyot as 64 [ס״ד], and the wings of the four Ḥayyot as 256 [נו״ר]. The meaning of the verse would be: ״וארבעה פנים לאחד״ — to each and every Ḥayah four faces, and each and every face is squared (מרובעת), so there are sixteen faces that each and every Ḥayah has, and it stands with four faces in every direction of the four directions of the world — so they are 64 [ס״ד] faces for all four. And what it says, ״וארבע פנים לאחת להן״, means for each and every face. And since each and every Ḥayah has sixteen faces and they are four faces for each and every face, there are 64 [ס״ד] wings that one Ḥayah has; and likewise each and every Ḥayah has 64 [ס״ד]: the Ḥayah of the east 64 [ס״ד], and the Ḥayah of the west 64 [ס״ד], and likewise north and south — the wings of the four Ḥayyot are found to number 256 [נו״ר]. Your sign is (Daniel 7:9): ״גלגלוהי נו״ר דליק״. And so Jonathan translated: sixteen wings for each and every face, sixty-four wings for one Ḥayah, so that the count of wings of four Ḥayyot is two hundred and fifty-six wings. And when the Temple was destroyed, the wings of the Ḥayyot were diminished, as the Sages, blessed be their memory, expounded in Ḥagigah, chapter Ein Dorshin: one verse says (Isaiah 6:2) ״שש כנפים לאחד״, and one verse says (Ezekiel 1:6) ״וארבע כנפים לאחת להן״ — no contradiction: here, when the Temple stands; here, when the Temple does not stand. As it were, the wings of the Ḥayyot were diminished. Which of them were diminished? Rav Ḥananel said in the name of Rav: those with which they would song — it is written here (Isaiah 6:2) ״ובשתים יעופף״, and it is written there (Proverbs 23:5) ״התעיף עיניך בו ואיננו״. The Rabbis say: those with which they would cover their feet, as stated (Ezekiel 1:7) ״ורגליהם רגל ישרה״. If so, from which was the diminution known? Up to here. The number of wings that were diminished was eight, for each and every Ḥayah two; and according to the Rabbis, they are those with which they would cover their feet, and this was in order to recall the sin of the Calf. And there remained for them 248 [רמ״ח] wings with which they minister to the Holy One, blessed be He, and recite song, corresponding to the 248 [רמ״ח] commandments we were commanded to serve HaShem, exalted be He, through them. And corresponding to those eight that were diminished, we have the commandment of circumcision on the eighth, and it protects us in all generations." "<b>And you should understand.</b> That the Holy One, blessed be He, intended in the creation of man that he be upon the earth like one of the angels in heaven or like the holy Ḥayyot. Now man is one body, and from it extend special limbs, like the image of the body of a tree from which branches and leaves extend — so from the body of man extend four special limbs, so that man would be a Chariot like the holy Ḥayyot and the Shekhinah would rest upon him. They are: the brain, the heart, the covenant of the tongue, and the covenant of circumcision. Therefore he was commanded in the wrapping of the ṭallit, that the four wings of the ṭallit overshadow the Ḥayah, as it is written (Deuteronomy 22): ״גדילים תעשה לך על ארבעה כנפות כסותך״. And this is the secret of the language that it mentioned in this commandment the word כנפים, when it could have said צדי or רבועי. And likewise it says here, ״על כנפי בגדיהם״, ״הכנף פתיל תכלת״. And in order to allude to the count of wings and faces that each and every Ḥayah has, the Kabbalah comes that there be eight threads in each wing, so that they be thirty-two threads. And just as the Ḥayah, which is divided into four Ḥayyot, is overshadowed by the Glory and the Shekhinah of His might, so the body of man, from which the four limbs we mentioned are divided, was commanded in the ṭallit that overshadows us, that the ṣiẓit be white and that he place in it a thread of tekhelet, so that it resemble the Ḥayyot of the Chariot. Now the white is the principal, and this is what they said: he begins with white and concludes with white, for it is in the secret of the teru'ah, which has a simple blast before it and a simple blast after it." "<b>And behold.</b> The ṣiẓit and the tekhelet are in the image of the lulav and the etrog, and just as the lulav and the etrog are mutually indispensible, so the ṣiẓit and the tekhelet are mutually indispensible at a time when tekhelet was available; but now that we do not have tekhelet, one may use the white without tekhelet, for it is the principal. And behold, the tekhelet corresponds to the etrog, which is called hadar." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> In the Midrash on Psalms (Psalms 90): ״והדרך על בניהם״ — and this derekh is the tekhelet of the commandment of ṣiẓit, with which Israel are covered, for they are called sons to the Place. And they further expounded: ṣiẓit has no measure upward, but it has a measure downward; and likewise they expounded concerning the lulav: lulav has no measure upward, but it has a measure downward. And in order that a person not separate between the ṣiẓit and the tekhelet, and not cut among the plantings, the Sages, blessed be their memory, needed to expound: ״ולא תתורו אחרי לבבכם״ — this is heresy; that is, that one not speculate concerning it to say there are two authorities, but rather unify a complete unity in all." "<b>And further.</b> I add an explanation: if you look at the forceful verse that Solomon said (Proverbs 24): ״ירא את ה׳ בני ומלך״, whose meaning is on this path: ״ירא את ה׳״ — this is the commandment of ṣiẓit; ״ומלך״ — this is the tekhelet, which is the attribute of Malkhut and the culmination of the Sefirot. And in order that you unify a complete unity and not cut, it says ״עם שונים אל תתערב״ — do not think duality in your heart, but rather a complete unity. And this is what the Sages, blessed be their memory, expounded: ״ולא תתורו אחרי לבבכם״ — this is heresy. According to the matter mentioned, ״וראיתם אותו״ would be from the language of ot (sign) and token, as if it said: ״וראיתם אותו״ — and you shall see His seal. And it is the seal of the tekhelet. And if you merit, you will understand." "<b>And in the Book</b> of the Bahir you will find it explicitly stated: What is the reason for tekhelet? It is compared to what? To a king and his daughter who sought to go far away. They feared the king's awe — he gave them signs. They feared the daughter — she gave them a sign. They said: henceforth, with these two signs, ״יהוה שומרך מכל רע ישמור את נפשך״ — 'YHWH guard you from all evil; He will guard your soul.'" "<b>And in the Midrash</b> of Tanhuma, ״ועשו להם ציצית״ — as it is said (Isaiah 42:21): ״ה' חפץ למען צדקו יגדיל תורה ויאדיר״ — 'HaShem desired, for the sake of His righteousness, to magnify the Torah and make it glorious.' The Holy One, blessed be He, gave the Torah to Israel to bequeath them the life of the world to come, and He left nothing for which He did not give Israel a commandment. If he goes out to plow — He commanded him (Deuteronomy 22:10): ״לא תחרוש בשור ובחמור יחדו״. To sow — (Deuteronomy 22:9): ״לא תזרע כרמך כלאים״. To reap — (Deuteronomy 24:19): ״כי תקצור קצירך בשדך ושכחת עומר בשדה לא תשוב לקחתו״. To thresh — terumah and tithes. To knead — (Numbers 15:20): ״ראשית עריסותיכם חלה תרימו״. To eat — he blesses over his sustenance. To shear — (Deuteronomy 18:4): ״וראשית גז צאנך תתן לו״. She gives birth — he gives the firstborn to the priest. To slaughter — (Deuteronomy 18:3): ״ונתן לכהן הזרוע והלחיים והקיבה״. (Deuteronomy 22:6): ״כי יקרא קן צפור״ — שלח תשלח. He slaughters a beast or a bird — (Leviticus 17:13): ״ושפך את דמו וכסהו בעפר״. He comes to clothe himself — (Deuteronomy 22:11): ״לא תלבש שעטנז״. A son is born to him — he circumcises him, and he guards from his wife at known times. He plants saplings — (Leviticus 19:23): ״וערלתם ערלתו״. He buries his dead — (Deuteronomy 14): ״לא תתגודדו״. He shaves his hair — (Leviticus 19:23): ״לא תקיפו״. He builds a house — (Deuteronomy 22:8): ״ועשית מעקה לגגך״. He sets up doors — (Deuteronomy 6:9): ״וכתבתם על מזוזות ביתך ובשעריך״. He wraps himself in his tallit — ״ועשו להם ציצית״. A parable: to a man drowning in water. What did the helmsman do? He extended the rope to him and said to him: 'Grasp the rope in your hand and do not let it go, for if you let it go you have no life.' So too here, the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Israel: As long as you are cleaving to the commandments, you have life, as it is said (Deuteronomy 4:4): ״ואתם הדבקים בה' אלהיכם חיים כלכם היום״. End."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {6 "The citation '(דניאל י״ב:ו׳)' appended to the Bereshit Rabbah reference appears misplaced in the source; it is the verse ויאמר לאיש לבוש הבדים from Daniel 12:6, likely a marginal gloss absorbed into the text. The gematria chain (ס״ד=64, תר״ם=640, minus כ״ז=27, yielding תרי״ג=613) is reproduced exactly as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:34:26.236995756Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}